  [PASS] 4a THE ANSWER TO THE QUESTION AS POSED: grouping by measurement method does NOT organise the table better than acceleration.  It does lower the raw rms -- seven extra free parameters always do -- but it loses on AIC to BOTH the one-parameter acceleration slope and to doing nothing at all, and a permutation of the method labels reproduces its tightness one time in three   (method: 8 params, rms 0.4353 dex, AIC -47.2; log y_bar: 2 params, rms 0.4837, AIC -51.2; constant: 1 param, rms 0.4929, AIC -51.8; permutation p(method) = 0.336)
  |B| vs log y_bar on this table (keepers included): Spearman rho = -0.251, permutation p = 0.1264
  [PASS] 4b AGAINST INTEREST, and this weakens the previous phase's headline too: once the KEEPERS are put into the same table on the same axes, the acceleration ordering of |B| is itself weakened.  It is still the best single organiser in the table, but the keepers sit at y_bar = 1e-3 to 0.24 with |B| < 0.03 while liabilities at the SAME y_bar reach 0.42-1.20 dex   (rho(|B|, log y) = -0.251 (p = 0.126) on N = 38 including keepers; u01's pressure-only table gave -0.636 (p = 0.014) on 15 rows with no keeper in it.  No single continuous axis organises the full table: log y, log M and log r explain 0.037, 0.042 and 0.070 of the variance of signed B)

