      vertical_stellar_kin   N = 1 -- too few rows for a slope
  permutation control on E7: shuffling the method labels and refitting all 16 parameters gives RSS <= the real one in 1.5% of 2000 shuffles (real 3.417, shuffled median 5.928)
  [PASS] 5h AGAINST MY OWN FRAMING, and this is the one place the angle produced something real.  E7 does NOT fail the way I wrote it down before running it: sixteen parameters DO win on AIC (delta -7.8) and the structure IS significant against a relabelling of the methods (p = 0.015).  Three computed facts stop that from rescuing the measurement hypothesis.  (i) It loses on BIC by +16.8 -- 16 parameters on 38 rows.  (ii) The improvement is carried by the SLOPES and not by the OFFSETS, and a calibration bias is a CONSTANT: one shared offset plus per-method slopes, which contains no measurement freedom at all, recovers 0.39 of the variance where the offsets-only model recovers 0.22.  (iii) DECISIVELY, the two slopes doing the work are -0.62 (resolved stellar RVs) and +0.60 (discrete tracers), and those two groups are exactly the positive-dwarf and the negative-star-cluster families -- so E7 is the halo/no-halo split written as slopes, and the 2-PARAMETER halo split beats E7 on AIC by 10.8 with fourteen fewer parameters   (E7 AIC -59.53 vs constant -51.77 (E7 wins by 7.8); E7 BIC -33.33 vs -50.14 (E7 loses by 16.8); E7 permutation p = 0.015 (SIGNIFICANT -- reported against my own framing); variance explained slopes-only 0.390 on 9 params vs offsets-only 0.220 on 8; the 2-parameter halo/no-halo split reaches AIC -70.28, +10.7 better than E7)
  what that decomposition MEANS, stated plainly: the significant structure in this table is a dependence of the offset on ACCELERATION that differs from class to class -- a statement about physics or about sample selection, not about how the velocity was measured.  Method and system class are almost perfectly confounded in this ledger (each method is one or two object families), so 'method x acceleration' cannot be separated from 'class x acceleration' -- and the class reading is the cheaper one by fourteen parameters.

==============================================================================================================================
6.  WHAT MEASUREMENT DOES EXPLAIN -- reported because it is real, and it runs against the framework
==============================================================================================================================
    hydrostatic masses at R500:        median B = +0.170   (['X-COP clusters at 0.5 R500', 'X-COP clusters at 0.9 R500', 'X-ray groups at R500 (Lovisari+2015)'])
    weak-lensing-calibrated at R500:   median B = +0.337
  [PASS] 6a THE ONE REAL MEASUREMENT EFFECT IN THE TABLE, and it costs the framework rather than saving it: at the SAME radius (R500) the mass technique matters, and the technique that reads LOW is the one that produces the framework's two best cluster rows.  Undoing the measured 20% hydrostatic bias closes most of that gap in the direction of the worse number   (weak-lensing R500 median +0.337 vs hydrostatic R500 median +0.170: +0.167 dex; the b = 0.20 correction supplies +0.048 dex of it (29%))

==============================================================================================================================
7.  MUTATION CONTROLS
==============================================================================================================================
  [PASS] M1 POWER: in a synthetic world where the measurement hypothesis is TRUE -- the same 38 rows, the same eight method labels, real per-method offsets spanning +-0.45 dex and a 0.12 dex within-method scatter -- the machinery detects it essentially every time, on AIC and BIC together and by permutation.  So section 4's null is a MEASUREMENT that the structure is absent, not a failure of the estimator   (method model wins on AIC and BIC in 200/200 synthetic trials; permutation p < 0.05 in 200/200; on the REAL table it wins in neither (AIC -47.2 vs -51.8, permutation p = 0.336))
  [PASS] M2 a wrong a_0 breaks the keeper the shift tests are run against: pushing a_0 up by a decade moves the SPARC RAR's mean residual far off zero, so the RAR keeper is a real constraint and not an identity   (a_0 x 10: RAR mean residual +0.0124 -> -0.3852 dex, rms 0.1764 -> 0.1984)
  [PASS] M3 shuffling the offsets against the method labels reproduces the observed within-method scatter: the real grouping is no tighter than a random one   (observed within-method RSS 7.199; shuffled median 7.616, fraction of shuffles at least as tight 0.341)

==============================================================================================================================
8.  THE ALTERNATIVE COMPUTED BESIDE
==============================================================================================================================
  Under LambdaCDM the same table is not a table of failures at all: every positive row is a system
  with a dark halo and every negative row is a system without one.  That reading was already made in
  u01_pressure_supported (its section 6).  What THIS script adds is that the reading survives the
  measurement question: the split by 'has a dark halo' is not a split by measurement technique.
    'has a LambdaCDM dark halo' grouping: 2 params, rms 0.3763 dex, AIC -70.28, permutation p over random 6-vs-32 splits = 0.0002
    measurement-method grouping:          8 params, rms 0.4353 dex, AIC -47.22, permutation p = 0.3360
    CAVEAT, stated because it is the whole weight of this section: the 'no halo' set is four outer-halo
    globular clusters plus DF2 and DF4, and it is very close to being 'the negative rows'.  The
    classification is inherited from u01_pressure_supported, which states it was fixed before its
    offsets were read -- but EXTENDING it to all 38 rows here is mine and is partly hindsight.  It is
    reported as what the numbers say, at its own significance, and not as a result.
  [PASS] 8a the LambdaCDM-shaped grouping ('does this system have a dark halo in the standard picture?') beats the eight-way measurement grouping on this table with six fewer parameters and at a permutation significance the method grouping never reaches -- reported because it is what the numbers say, not because it is the framework's friend, and carrying the hindsight caveat above   (halo grouping AIC -70.28 on 2 params (permutation p 0.0002) vs method AIC -47.22 on 8 params (permutation p 0.3360))

==============================================================================================================================
VERDICT
==============================================================================================================================
  ANGLE E IS ANSWERED IN THE NEGATIVE.  The liability ledger is NOT one measurement systematic, and
  it does not need rewriting on measurement grounds.

  1. Grouping by measurement method costs 7 free parameters and lowers the rms from 0.493 to 0.435 dex.
     It loses to the constant on AIC by 4.6 and to a one-parameter acceleration slope by 4.0; permutation p on
     the method label = 0.336.  The same machinery detects a synthetic method offset in 200/200 trials, so this is a null,
     not a blind estimator.
  2. Three of the eight methods contain rows of BOTH signs.  A calibration offset is one number per
     method and cannot produce two signs.  The largest method group -- dispersions from individually
     resolved stellar radial velocities -- spans +1.65 to -0.78 dex on its own, against a total table spread of 2.80.
  3. THE DECISIVE SINGLE FACT is inside ONE method at MATCHED acceleration: the KiDS isolated-dwarf
     lens stack (lensing, y_bar 1.0e-03) returns a_0 to 0.009 dex, while eRASS1 group lensing
     masses (lensing, y_bar 4.2e-3) need +0.42 dex.  Same technique, 0.6 decades apart in acceleration,
     0.42 dex apart in offset.  Neither method nor acceleration organises the lensing family.
  4. THE KEEPER BREAK, computed on the files and not asserted.  The offset the lensing liabilities
     demand, applied to the KiDS data, moves the isolated-dwarf a_0 from 9.55e-11 to 1.23e-11,
     -0.88 dex off the canonical footing.  The 1/r law SURVIVES (a constant factor cannot move a log
     slope) and so do Renzo at first order and the inner-diversity ratio (they are ratios inside one
     curve).  The a_0 METERS do not: the lensing one and, under the rotation offset, the deep tail.
  5. E7, the maximal measurement model, is the one place I was wrong before running it, and the
     correction is reported rather than buried.  Sixteen parameters DO win on AIC (-59.5 vs -51.8) and the
     structure IS significant against a relabelling of the methods (p = 0.015).  But it loses on BIC by
     16.8; the improvement is carried by the per-method SLOPES (0.39 of the variance on 9 parameters) and not
     by the OFFSETS (0.22 on 8), and a calibration bias is a constant; and the two slopes doing the
     work, -0.62 for resolved stellar RVs and +0.60 for discrete tracers, are exactly the positive-dwarf
     and negative-star-cluster families.  E7 is the halo/no-halo split written as slopes, and the
     2-parameter halo split beats it on AIC by 10.7 with fourteen fewer parameters.  Method and
     system class are confounded throughout this ledger and cannot be separated by it.
  6. The one measurement effect that IS present -- hydrostatic versus weak-lensing masses at R500, +0.167 dex
     -- runs AGAINST the framework: undoing the measured 20% hydrostatic bias removes its two best
     cluster rows.  Zero liabilities fixed, one made worse, and b is measured rather than free.
  7. The veins workflow's velocity-measurement organiser does not transfer.  Zero liability rows are
     unresolved-line-width measurements; it organises the a_0 LADDER, where line widths and resolved
     curves measure the same thing in the same galaxies, not this ledger.

  WHAT THIS ANGLE ADDS, BOTH WAYS:
  * AGAINST THE PREVIOUS PHASE: the acceleration ordering of |B| was measured on tables with no
    KEEPERS in them.  With the four recomputed keepers included, rho(|B|, log y) falls to -0.251 (p = 0.126).
    Keepers sit at y_bar 1e-3 to 0.24 with |B| < 0.03 while liabilities at the SAME y_bar reach
    +0.42 to +1.20 dex.  On signed B no continuous axis reaches R^2 = 0.08: log y 0.037, log M 0.042, log r 0.070.
  * FOR THE FRAMEWORK: the two keepers most exposed to this angle survive it.  The 1/r lensing law and
    Renzo's rule are shift-blind by construction, so no calibration story of any size touches them.
  * THE HONEST RESIDUAL: what organises the table is a class-dependent dependence on acceleration.
    A two-way split on 'does this system have a dark halo in the standard picture' reaches rms 0.376 dex
    on two parameters (permutation p = 0.0002), better than eight methods reach on eight.  That is the
    LambdaCDM-shaped reading, it is partly hindsight here, and it is reported at its own significance.

